  <a href="#section-configure" id="section-configure" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Configure the relationship</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">FailoverPolicy policy = FailoverPolicy.builder()
    .withPrimaryRecheckInterval(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
    .build();

try (Mailer alternative = mail.mailerBuilder()
         .withSMTPServer("secondary.example.org", 587)
         .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
         .buildMailer();
     Mailer primary = mail.mailerBuilder()
         .withSMTPServer("primary.example.org", 587)
         .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
         .withFailoverMailer(alternative, policy)
         .buildMailer()) {
    primary.async().sendMail(email).getCompletion().join();
}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>The same relationship works with <code>primary.sync().sendMail(email)</code>, with or without the batch module.
        Pooling reuses connections; it is not required to enable failover. Add each server's credentials and other settings to its own builder.</p>
      <p><code>withFailoverMailer(mailer, policy)</code> replaces the destination and policy together.
        <code>clearFailoverMailer()</code> removes both, unless centrally supplied configuration locks prevent that change.</p>
      <p>You can give B its own relationship to C. One operation follows that route with one deadline, cancellation control, completion and observer.
        It never recursively calls B's public send method or moves the job into B's executor.</p>
      <p>Supplied Mailers are borrowed: closing A does not close B. Close them yourself after their work has drained, as the example does.
        Mailers privately built from named properties are owned and closed by their parent. A participant cannot close itself from a send callback
        that is still using it. A foreign implementation of the <code>Mailer</code> interface cannot be composed this way; an SJM-created Mailer
        using another Jakarta Mail provider can, where the provider exposes enough evidence to identify connection failures.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-ownership" id="section-ownership" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Which Mailer owns what?</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Suppose you call A and the selected connection belongs to B. B's settings matter for more than its hostname.</p>
    <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable failover ownership matrix"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>Owner</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Executor, admission, observer, result, cancellation and overall deadline</td><td>A, the initiating Mailer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ordinary Email defaults/overrides, validation and message protection</td><td>B, the actual selected Mailer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Credentials, TLS, proxy, Session, provider, size and content settings</td><td>B</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sending allowance</td><td>The selected SMTP configuration; switching destinations doesn't reset its history</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shared cluster pool settings</td><td>The existing first-registration owner</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mandatory message locks</td><td>A, traversed failover Mailers, and the selected cluster member together</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Connection-setting locks</td><td>The actual approved destination</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p>Each candidate starts from your original Email. If A normally adds <code>X-Relay: primary</code> and B adds
      <code>X-Relay: secondary</code>, B gets only its own ordinary template. A's ordinary BCC recipients do not travel with it either.
      This selected-member rule also applies to cluster load balancing without failover.</p>
    <p>B can have a maintainer BCC of its own, so staff get a copy only when B sends. That is an actual recipient: consider confidentiality,
      recipient limits and cost. A mandatory archive BCC supplied through <code>simplejavamail.locked.defaults.bcc.address</code> still applies,
      in addition to B's ordinary BCC. Conflicting mandatory message settings stop the operation.</p>
    <p>Defaults, overrides and per-property suppression keep their normal precedence within the selected Mailer. Exact emails still bypass ordinary
      templates, and their authoritative bytes are not rewritten. If mandatory message settings cannot be honored without altering protected or exact
      content, the operation fails. See <a href="/configuration.html">configuration</a> and <a href="/features.html#section-exact-eml">exact EML submission</a>.</p>
    <p>A <code>CustomMailer</code> remains an opaque callback and is never invoked again automatically. Logging-only sends don't try SMTP connections.
      Do not configure either as a delivery fallback and assume it proves SMTP acceptance.</p>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-policy" id="section-policy" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Choose failure modes and recovery timing</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable failover policy reference"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Builder method</th><th>Default</th><th>What it changes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td><code>withPrimaryConnectionAttempts(n)</code></td><td>1</td><td>Total setup attempts per candidate during this visit, including the first</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withPrimaryConnectionRetryDelay(duration)</code></td><td>Zero</td><td>Wait between additional attempts you explicitly enable</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withPrimaryRecheckInterval(duration)</code></td><td>30 seconds</td><td>When a previously failed destination becomes eligible for another check</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withPrimaryRecheckBackoff(multiplier, maximum)</code></td><td>Disabled</td><td>Increase that interval once per failed recheck, up to the supplied maximum</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withFailoverOnAuthenticationFailure(true)</code></td><td>false</td><td>Allow another destination after credentials are rejected</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>withFailoverOnCertificateVerificationFailure(true)</code></td><td>false</td><td>Allow another destination after certificate or server-identity checks fail</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p>Every policy setting has a matching reset method. Read the resolved policy through
      <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig().getFailoverPolicy()</code>; it is absent when no relationship is configured.</p>
    <p>Extra connection attempts don't skip recovery steps. With three attempts and a 30-second initial interval, the first recheck
      is still due 30 seconds after the last failed attempt. A failed recheck increases the next interval once, even if it tried connecting three times.</p>
    <p>For example, an expired certificate on A need not stop mail if your deployment permits another independently trusted relay.
      Enable certificate-failure failover explicitly. A still fails verification, and B must pass its own checks. This setting does not trust
      expired certificates, turn off hostname verification or downgrade mandatory TLS. Authentication failure is a separate decision.</p>
    <p>By default, eligible failures include DNS lookup failure, connection refusal, unreachable endpoints, setup timeouts/disconnects and
      temporary service-unavailable setup replies. Invalid local configuration, message validation, lock conflicts and unmet required capabilities
      stop the send. So do unknown setup failures, ordinary pool-capacity timeouts, cancellation, shutdown and the overall deadline.</p>
    <p>With the default one-attempt policy, A fails and B is considered immediately. Working B continues under its own sending limits while A waits
      for reconsideration. When A is due, one operation tries it; other eligible operations can continue through B. A successful setup restores its
      preference. Background pool replenishment does not keep opening new connections to A during that interval, and no health-probe executor is added.</p>
    <p>Recheck observations are local to the factory and configured destination identity, not shared by hostname or across processes. Repeated construction
      from one named definition shares the relevant history and allowance. Separate factories remain independent. A permissive caller's policy never
      grants a stricter caller permission to skip authentication or certificate failure.</p>
    <p>In a cluster, the selection strategy still chooses the first member. Eligible connection failures can exhaust the captured group before following
      the configured failover relationship. A busy but healthy member retains its normal waiting behavior. Neither new registrations nor retiring ones
      make this send's candidate traversal unbounded.</p>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-named" id="section-named" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Configure named destinations and locks</h2>
  <section><div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.smtp.host=primary.example.org
simplejavamail.smtp.port=587
simplejavamail.transportstrategy=SMTP_TLS

simplejavamail.failover.mailer=secondary
simplejavamail.failover.primary.connectionattempts=1
simplejavamail.failover.primary.retrydelay=PT0S
simplejavamail.failover.primary.recheckinterval=PT30S
simplejavamail.failover.primary.backoffmultiplier=1
simplejavamail.failover.primary.maximumrecheckinterval=PT30S
simplejavamail.failover.onauthenticationfailure=false
simplejavamail.failover.oncertificateverificationfailure=false

simplejavamail.mailers.secondary.smtp.host=secondary.example.org
simplejavamail.mailers.secondary.smtp.port=587
simplejavamail.mailers.secondary.transportstrategy=SMTP_TLS
simplejavamail.mailers.secondary.defaults.bcc.address=mail-operations@example.org</code></pre>
  </div><div class="wide">
    <p>Named definitions use the existing property tails and resolve independently. Secondary does not inherit primary's ordinary settings or credentials.
      For a further relationship, set <code>simplejavamail.mailers.secondary.failover.mailer=third</code> and define
      <code>simplejavamail.mailers.third.*</code>. Missing references, cycles and known conflicting locks are rejected before private resources are built.</p>
    <p>A multiplier of one means fixed reconsideration intervals. A larger multiplier requires an explicit maximum. Policy properties without
      <code>failover.mailer</code> are incomplete configuration and are rejected.</p>
    <p>The same names work through the <a href="/spring.html">Spring Environment integration</a>, including nested YAML. A central configuration owner
      can lock the route and a destination's TLS settings:</p>
  </div><div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.locked.failover.mailer=secondary
simplejavamail.locked.mailers.secondary.transportstrategy=SMTP_TLS
simplejavamail.locked.defaults.bcc.address=archive@example.org</code></pre>
  </div><div class="wide">
    <p>A locked relationship protects the captured permitted definition and its nested relationships, not just the text “secondary”. Java code cannot
      substitute a different Mailer with that label. Policy fields can be locked separately. A's locked SMTP host stays A's host; choosing approved B
      does not edit A's connection configuration. Locks prevent accidental changes within this factory, not deliberate use of an unrelated factory.</p>
    <p>The CLI uses generated named-selection options. Put the named definitions in your loaded properties and select a route with
      <code>--mailer:withFailoverMailerName secondary</code>, optionally adding
      <code>--mailer:withFailoverPrimaryRecheckInterval PT30S</code>. The command still waits for its send to finish.
      See <a href="/cli.html">CLI configuration</a>; Java callers use the paired Mailer/policy API, not these internal text-based methods.</p>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-batches" id="section-batches" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Recover during a large batch</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>A lazy batch need not finish thousands of emails on B after A has recovered. It can reconsider A between emails:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text small">Email 1: A cannot connect; B connects and submits it.
Emails 2–40: B keeps its connection and sends under B's rate rules.
Before email 41: A is due for a recheck and connects successfully.
Emails 41 onward: A sends under A's rate rules.</code></pre>
    <p>The current email and its required observer handoff finish before switching. The iterator is opened once and is never restarted. Only untouched
      emails move to A; none is cancelled and resubmitted mid-send. If A's recheck fails, the current eligible B connection can remain in use.</p>
    <p>The first message failure still stops the batch. A successful connection doesn't allow automatic recovery from a submission failure.
      A scoped <code>withOpenConnection</code> callback behaves differently: initial acquisition may fail over, then that callback stays pinned to its
      selected connection and is invoked exactly once. Shared setup and final teardown are not charged repeatedly to each email's diagnostic timings.</p>
    <p>Its sender belongs to that callback's thread and cannot be used after the callback ends. Don't hand it to another thread; use
      <code>mailer.async().sendMail(...)</code> for independent asynchronous sends.</p>
    <p>With failover configured and the batch module present, both scopes retain a lease from the selected pool. It occupies pool capacity during
      the batch or callback, including application work between sends, and is released or invalidated when the scope ends. A healthy release need not
      close the socket. Without the batch module, the scope owns and closes a direct connection. Without configured failover, these APIs keep their
      dedicated, non-pooled behavior even when the batch module is present.</p>
    <p>Don't wait inside a callback or inline batch observer for another send that needs the same exhausted pool. For example, a size-one pool cannot
      lend a second connection while this scope retains its only lease. Use the callback's sender, or submit that independent work after the scope ends.</p>
    <p>Retained connections can sit idle during per-email sending-limit waits. Configure server idle timeouts accordingly; failover does not silently
      reconnect a pinned callback or bypass rate limits. See the <a href="/email-workload-field-guide.html">email workload field guide</a> for choosing the sending shape.</p>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-observation" id="section-observation" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>See what happened</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>New connection incidents are logged at ERROR, meaningful failover/recovery at INFO, and repeated failed rechecks at DEBUG.
      Factory-local suppression keeps each send from reporting the same outage as a new incident. A successful failover still produces a successful
      send outcome, not a separate application failure callback.</p>
    <p>With a <code>MailSendObserver</code>, an individual send also records its connection route:</p>
  </div><div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">.withMailSendObserver(outcome -> outcome.getDiagnostics().ifPresent(report -> {
    report.getConnectionHistory().forEach(step -> {
        System.out.printf("attempted=%s acquired=%s elapsed=%s detail=%s%n",
            step.isConnectionAttempted(), step.isConnectionAcquired(),
            step.getElapsed(), step.getExplanation());
    });
    System.out.println("omitted steps=" + report.getOmittedConnectionSteps());
}))</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-text small">attempted=true  acquired=false elapsed=Optional[PT0.02S] detail=Optional[Connection unavailable]
attempted=true  acquired=true  elapsed=Optional[PT0.08S] detail=Optional.empty
omitted steps=0</code></pre>
  </div><div class="wide">
    <p>A later send might instead show <code>attempted=false</code> for A: it was skipped because of an earlier failure, not contacted again.
      History retains the first 63 steps and the latest step; the omitted count describes anything in between. This reporting bound never limits routing.
      Optional configured-name and logical host/port getters let your application add endpoint labels deliberately. Automatic rendering excludes them.</p>
    <p>Async scheduling includes admission and executor waiting. Preparation starts during execution, after selection, and accumulates governance and
      validation across candidates. Total elapsed time includes orchestration and retry delays, so the measurements need not sum to the total.
      <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-send-timings">Send diagnostics</a> explain the other boundaries.</p>
    <p>For a separate diagnostic check, probe A or B explicitly with <code>mailer.sync().probeConnection(true)</code>. Probing answers whether that
      configured server can connect now; it does not follow the send failover chain or spend sending allowance.</p>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-retry" id="section-retry" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Schedule message retries in your application</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Connection failover and a later message retry solve different problems. Use your job system to decide when to try again, keep a bounded attempt
      count and backoff, and correlate the outcome to the original job. An observer receives an outcome, not the original Email or a durable job.</p>
    <p>This sketch hands work to an application queue. <code>jobs</code> and <code>retryQueue</code> are application services, not library APIs:</p>
  </div><div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">.withMailSendObserver(outcome -> {
    if (outcome.isSuccessful()) {
        return;
    }
    outcome.getSubmissionReceipt().ifPresent(receipt -> {
        switch (receipt.getRetryDisposition()) {
            case SAFE_TO_RETRY_ALL:
            case SAFE_TO_RETRY_UNACCEPTED:
                retryQueue.scheduleAfterBackoff(
                    jobs.findByMessageId(outcome.getEffectiveMessageId()),
                    receipt.getRetryableRecipients());
                break;
            default:
                // Unknown acceptance, permanent rejection or another policy decision:
                // don't automatically submit the original message again.
                break;
        }
    });
})</code></pre>
  </div><div class="wide">
    <p>Build any later submission for only the recipients the job policy permits. Never blindly replay a partially accepted message, and stop automatic
      retries when acceptance is uncertain. Also check locks and template-added recipients: a mandatory archive BCC can receive another copy even when
      the application's recipient subset excludes already accepted addresses. Read the <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-retry-decisions">receipt guidance</a>.</p>
    <p>No receipt is not itself permission to retry. Local validation/configuration errors need fixing; exhausted connection routes may warrant delayed
      rescheduling after the underlying cause has been assessed. An accepted receipt followed by cleanup failure must not become a duplicate send.</p>
    <p>Keep inline observer work short: enqueue the decision and return. Alternatively use the existing observer executor option. Do not sleep or resend
      synchronously inside the callback. Simple Java Mail supplies neither a durable retry queue nor an automatic message-retry policy here.</p>
  </div></section>
